          "prompt": "Stylized hand-painted 3D game environment art for a fast first-person roguelite shooter, matching the\nreadable visual direction of Gunfire Reborn: bold chunky silhouettes, broad clean faceted planes, two or three dominant\ncolour blocks, soft painted gradients and a few restrained edge accents. East-Asian Silk Road frontier fantasy of the Han-dynasty Gobi desert: ochre-red sandstone and wind-carved yardang rock with horizontal strata, pale sand-yellow rammed earth with reed layers, weathered grey-brown timber, and only a few faded Dunhuang mural accents in malachite green, lapis blue and vermilion. Matte stone,\nwood and cloth, restrained highlights on metal, sparse painted wear. No photorealism, micro-grain, dense tiny cracks,\nelaborate miniature carvings, harsh specular highlights or baked cast shadows. Even neutral lighting.\n\nAsset (2m wide x 2.6m tall x 2m deep): A large Tang-dynasty temple courtyard bronze incense burner 2.6m tall: a low square stone pedestal 2m wide and 0.4m tall; on it a big round-bellied bronze cauldron 1.6m wide standing on three thick stubby cabriole legs ending in lion paws, with two chunky upright loop handles on the rim; above it a separate tiered bronze lid shaped like a small pavilion roof with upturned corners, held up by four short thick posts so there are open gaps between the cauldron rim and the lid, topped by a fat round knob finial. Dark aged bronze with warm brown-gold highlights and soft verdigris-green patches, a few broad simplified cloud-scroll reliefs on the belly; ochre-red sandstone pedestal. PROPORTIONS: all four views nearly identical, drawn about 1.3 times as tall as wide. Broad faceted hand-painted planes, chunky forms, no smoke, incense sticks, ash or fine ornament. One solid connected piece on a flat base.\n\nOutput: one orthographic model turnaround sheet for 3D reconstruction. Exactly four separate complete views\nof the SAME object in one row, left to right:\n1. FRONT view: the object's front faces the viewer.\n2. LEFT side view: the object turned 90 degrees so its front points to the LEFT edge of the image (camera at the\n   object's right-hand side).\n3. BACK view: the object's back faces the viewer.\n4. RIGHT side view: the object turned 90 degrees so its front points to the RIGHT edge of the image.\nSame scale and same ground line in all four, each centred in its quarter with generous empty margin, never touching or\noverlapping; the whole object fits inside its quarter, nothing cropped.\nPlain flat light neutral grey background (#D6D6D6). No floor, no ground shadow, no text, no labels, no frames, no\nperspective.",
